    {
      "index": 2,
      "topicId": "64b16aecf23ac7d519f9b99a",
      "title": "Periodic properties",
      "totalQuestions": 5,
      "questions": [
        {
          "_id": "6a0be51644d287c9361e5cd2",
          "type": "multipleCorrect",
          "question": {
            "text": "The correct statement(s) regarding the periodic properties of elements is(are)",
            "image": null
          },
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "vQYA3",
              "text": "Second ionization enthalpy of carbon atom is less than that of boron atom.",
              "isCorrect": true,
              "image": null
            },
            {
              "id": "0LMkf",
              "text": "Increasing order of ionic radii: $\\text{Al}^{3+} < \\text{Mg}^{2+} < \\text{Na}^+$",
              "isCorrect": true,
              "image": null
            },
            {
              "id": "rmmfF",
              "text": "Under identical conditions, in solid state, the density of potassium metal is more than density of sodium metal.",
              "isCorrect": false,
              "image": null
            },
            {
              "id": "imG7v",
              "text": "The H$-$H bond is weaker than F$-$F bond.",
              "isCorrect": false,
              "image": null
            }
          ],
          "correctValue": null,
          "solution": {
            "text": "For (A): The electronic configuration of $\\text{B}^+$ is $1s^2 2s^2$ and that of $\\text{C}^+$ is $1s^2 2s^2 2p^1$. Removing an electron from the fully filled, more penetrating $2s$ orbital of $\\text{B}^+$ requires more energy than removing a $2p$ electron from $\\text{C}^+$. Thus, the second ionization enthalpy of carbon is less than that of boron. Statement (A) is correct.<br><br>For (B): $\\text{Al}^{3+}$, $\\text{Mg}^{2+}$, and $\\text{Na}^+$ are isoelectronic species with $10$ electrons each. The nuclear charge increases in the order $\\text{Na}^+ (11) < \\text{Mg}^{2+} (12) < \\text{Al}^{3+} (13)$. Higher effective nuclear charge leads to a stronger pull on the electrons, resulting in a smaller ionic radius. Thus, the order of ionic radii is $\\text{Al}^{3+} < \\text{Mg}^{2+} < \\text{Na}^+$. Statement (B) is correct.<br><br>For (C): The density of potassium ($0.86 \\text{ g/cm}^3$) is less than that of sodium ($0.97 \\text{ g/cm}^3$) due to an abnormal increase in atomic volume caused by the presence of empty $3d$ orbitals in potassium. Statement (C) is incorrect.<br><br>For (D): The bond dissociation enthalpy of $\\text{H}-\\text{H}$ ($436 \\text{ kJ/mol}$) is much higher than that of $\\text{F}-\\text{F}$ ($159 \\text{ kJ/mol}$) due to strong interelectronic repulsions between the lone pairs on the small fluorine atoms. Statement (D) is incorrect.<br><br>Answer: Second ionization enthalpy of carbon atom is less than that of boron atom.; Increasing order of ionic radii: $\\text{Al}^{3+} < \\text{Mg}^{2+} < \\text{Na}^+$",
            "image": null
          },
          "level": 2,
          "videoSolution": {
